(0) Exam 2B is easy and everyone gets between 45 and 51. The material is es=
sentially Set Theory in the guise of propositional calculus. Please look at=
 the chart comparing Sets, Propositional Calculus, and Boolean Algebra. Boo=
lean Algebras will again treat the material in Set Theory, in a new guise. =
Again Exam 2C is easy and everyone gets between 45 and 51,

(1) The connective-list and the truth-value number will make the exam diffe=
r from student to student. The Solved Exam does not have a connective-list =
or a truth-value list. All methods of solution will be found in the Solved =
Exam except for Problem (2b). The positive inspiration for Problem (2b) com=
es from one of Patrick Bacon's questions for which I thank him.

(2) (1a) is a trivial problem where one needs to find a truth-table.

(3) (1b) is also a trivial problem where one needs to find a truth-table to=
 decide if a certain connective distributes over another. The answer could =
be Y or N. The answer in the Solved Exam is N. If the answer is Y, then one=
 must box the entire column of 1's and refer to the entire boxed column.

(4) (1c) is a variation on (1b) and must agree with the answer to (1b). So =
if (1b) and (1c) are both attempted and the answers contradict each other, =
then no points will be awarded on either.

(5) (2a) is to find a formula with a given truth-table. The algorithm produ=
ces the formula in disjunctive normal form. An alternative canonical form, =
called conjunctive normal form, appears in (2b).

(6) (2b) is about Canonical Forms. Canonical Forms are useful in that they =
allow one to tell if two propositional terms are equivalent (or, as one loo=
sely says, equal). There are many canonical forms. (2b) asks one to produce=
 two canonical forms DNF and CNF. The algorithm for the DNF appears in the =
Notes. Two terms are equal if and only if they have the same DNF if and onl=
y if they have the same CNF.   In (2b) one is being asked to find both the =
DNF and the CNF. Since the DNF and CNF must have a truth-table complementar=
y to the one in Problem (1a)(i), one may cheat and determine which complete=
 expressions involving literals are involved. One, nevertheless, must verif=
y that the truth-tables are really what they should be, by actual evaluatio=
n. The two Wikipedia articles on Conjunctive normal forms and Disjunctive n=
ormal forms also describe algorithms that one may use.

(2c) is an exercise in PC (propositional calculus, not political correctnes=
s, the abominable infirmity of epidemic (fortunately not pandemic) proporti=
ons. It has been endemic to these shores for quite some time.

(3a) is a trivial problem of PC. Since the rules are stated in terms of not=
, or, and and, one must recast every expression in terms of these connectiv=
es.

(3b) is a not-entirely-trivial problem that needs to be done in terms of th=
e axioms and rules of inference, as in the Solved Exam. In case one uses so=
me other method, one will not get any credit. Please look at the Solved Exa=
m. In case the inference is not valid, then one must use the axioms and rul=
es of inference to deduce a contradiction.

(3c) allows one to use any method, for instance, the method of truth-tables=
.

(2) These problems will be solved using Boolean Algebra Methods on Exam (2C=
) which, one will discover, is infinitely easier and much closer to usual a=
lgebra that one has learnt in school.

