<td class=3D"maincell" style=3D"font-family:sans-serif; font-size:14px; ver=
tical-align:top; box-sizing:border-box; padding:20px;">
<p>(0) Exam 2B is easy and everyone gets between 45 and 51. The material is=
 essentially Set Theory in the guise of propositional calculus. Please look=
 at the chart comparing Sets, Propositional Calculus, and Boolean Algebra. =
Boolean Algebras will again treat
 the material in Set Theory, in a new guise. Again Exam 2C is easy and ever=
yone gets between 45 and 51,</p>
<p>(1) The connective-list and the truth-value number will make the exam di=
ffer from student to student. The Solved Exam does not have a connective-li=
st or a truth-value list. All methods of solution will be found in the Solv=
ed Exam except for Problem (2b).
 The positive inspiration for Problem (2b) comes from one of Patrick Bacon'=
s questions for which I thank him.</p>
<p>(2) (1a) is a trivial problem where one needs to find a truth-table.&nbs=
p;</p>
<p>(3) (1b) is also a trivial problem where one needs to find a truth-table=
 to decide if a certain connective distributes over another. The answer cou=
ld be Y or N. The answer in the Solved Exam is N. If the answer is Y, then =
one must box the entire column of
 1's and refer to the entire boxed column.</p>
<p>(4) (1c) is a variation on (1b) and must agree with the answer to (1b). =
So if (1b) and (1c) are both attempted and the answers contradict each othe=
r, then no points will be awarded on either.</p>
<p>(5) (2a) is to find a formula with a given truth-table. The algorithm pr=
oduces the formula in disjunctive normal form. An alternative canonical for=
m, called conjunctive normal form, appears in (2b).&nbsp;</p>
<p>(6) (2b) is about Canonical Forms. Canonical Forms are useful in that th=
ey allow one to tell if two propositional terms are equivalent (or, as one =
loosely says, equal). There are many canonical forms. (2b) asks one to prod=
uce two canonical forms DNF and
 CNF. The algorithm for the DNF appears in the Notes. Two terms are equal i=
f and only if they have the same DNF if and only if they have the same CNF.=
&nbsp; &nbsp;In (2b) one is being asked to find both the DNF and the CNF. S=
ince the DNF and CNF must have a truth-table
 complementary to the one in Problem (1a)(i), one may cheat and determine w=
hich complete expressions involving literals are involved. One, nevertheles=
s, must verify that the truth-tables are really what they should be, by act=
ual evaluation. The two Wikipedia
 articles on Conjunctive normal forms and Disjunctive normal forms also des=
cribe algorithms that one may use.&nbsp;</p>
<p>(2c) is an exercise in PC (propositional calculus, not political correct=
ness, the abominable infirmity of epidemic (fortunately not pandemic) propo=
rtions. It has been endemic to these shores for quite some time.&nbsp;</p>
<p>(3a) is a trivial problem of PC. Since the rules are stated in terms of =
not, or, and and, one must recast every expression in terms of these connec=
tives.&nbsp;</p>
<p>(3b) is a not-entirely-trivial problem that needs to be done in terms of=
 the axioms and rules of inference, as in the Solved Exam. In case one uses=
 some other method, one will not get any credit. Please look at the Solved =
Exam. In case the inference is not
 valid, then one must use the axioms and rules of inference to deduce a con=
tradiction.&nbsp;</p>
<p>(3c) allows one to use any method, for instance, the method of truth-tab=
les.</p>
<p>(2) These problems will be solved using Boolean Algebra Methods on Exam =
(2C) which, one will discover, is infinitely easier and much closer to usua=
l algebra that one has learnt in school.</p>
